[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked: a stable per-person id in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  Second question — this is the "works across the app" part.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how does a future form [3mget[0m the id into its call?

  - [1mShared module, explicit parameter at each call site.[0m [38;5;153msrc/identity.js[39m exports [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m; callers write
    [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, getUserId())[39m. The signature declares the dependency, tests can pass a fixed id with no
    mocking, and you can see at a glance which calls are tracked. Cost: every new form has to remember to pass it, and a
    forgotten one fails silently as [38;5;153mundefined[39m.
  - [1mShared module, read internally by each function.[0m Signatures stay as they are; [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m itself.
    Impossible to forget, nothing to thread through. Cost: a hidden global dependency that tests must stub, and it isn't
    a parameter — it walks back the thing you asked for.
  - [1mA submit-handler wrapper that injects it.[0m One [38;5;153mwithUserId(handler)[39m at the form-binding layer; new forms opt in by
    wrapping. Consistent by construction, but it's the most machinery for an app with one form today, and it only helps
    calls that originate from form submits.

  [1mMy recommendation: the shared module with an explicit parameter.[0m It's what you approved, it keeps [38;5;153mlogin()[39m a pure
  function of its inputs, and the "forgot to pass it" failure is cheap to catch — I'd have [38;5;153mlogin()[39m log a warning when
  [38;5;153muserId[39m is missing rather than let it go silently undefined. Option 2 is genuinely better at the "other forms won't
  forget" goal, so if that's your real worry, say so and I'll flip.
